WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:08.970
Let's play a game with

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.190
CuriousChimpanzee, who is a long time

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:14.590
viewer of the stream, and let's go for

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:15.490
it with 1e4.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.030
Yeah, as Jan said, I thought it was

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:19.790
more than impressive.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.390
Well, we have another chance to play an

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.530
Alapin, and we face d5.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:26.410
So I remember that previously I've

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.430
played this line against Curious, so

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:32.230
let's see if he has picked up some of

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:32.750
the theory.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.610
Of course, here we play e takes d5,

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.530
queen takes d5, and d4.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:39.490
We've had this particular position

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:42.410
many, many times, highly, highly theoretical.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:43.990
Black plays knight f6, which is the

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.770
main move, and of course, we respond by

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.610
developing our own knight, knight f3.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:51.470
This is all theory, and here, black is

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.270
at a major crossroads.

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:57.230
There are four or so options that are

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.970
all worthy of attention, and one of

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:01.950
them is indeed knight to c6.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.990
Now, Nc6 is probably the move that you

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.190
would be making if you were looking at

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.190
this for the first time from Black's perspective.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.090
The drawback of Nc6 is that the bishop

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:17.670
on f8 remains locked behind the e7 pawn.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:19.670
And according to the new Alep and

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:22.190
Chessable course, the best way to try

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:25.950
to punish the move Nc6 is precisely by

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:27.770
exploiting its principal drawback,

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.090
which is that when white plays DC,

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.250
black is unable to recapture on c5 with

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:33.770
the bishop.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.890
And if black has to take on c5 with the

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.590
queen, then it gives white a bunch of

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.750
tempi that we can use to accelerate our development.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:43.670
So the move here, according to the

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.390
chess book course, is pawn takes pawn.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:49.830
Now of course, pawn takes pawn is a

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.490
move that you might be reluctant to

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:53.010
play because it allows black to trade

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.190
queens and force our king out into the

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:56.430
center at the cost of a pawn.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.630
And you might also think that it's

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.890
quite easy to win the c5 pawn back, but

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:02.290
that couldn't be further from the truth.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.150
So I am going to play dc, even though

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:06.810
I'm pretty rusty on the theory after

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:07.530
queen takes d1.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.290
So if curious is intimately acquainted

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:13.170
with it, then, you know, then the

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:14.130
position is going to get sharp.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:15.050
a5.

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:18.630
So a5 is a very understandable move.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.610
He's preventing us from playing b4.

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:24.550
But that doesn't mean that we have to

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.670
part ways with the c5 pawn automatically.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.250
In fact, we have many, many ways that

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.710
we can try to cling to that pawn.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.370
We don't want to cling to it at the

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:34.650
cost of ruining our position.

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:36.690
We want to find some sort of middle ground.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:38.730
And if we're giving that pawn away,

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:40.250
then we want to find a good reason to

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:40.730
give it away.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.870
So what are the options for defending

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.210
this pawn straight away?

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.250
Well, one of them is to play bishop to e3.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.690
But as you know from many openings, the

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.050
drawback of bishop e3 is that it runs

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:53.730
into this very annoying move knight to

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:56.970
g4 Why is night g4 annoying because

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.650
night g4 threatens to eliminate the

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.270
bishop from the board to ruin our pawn

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:03.790
structure And then once black pushes e6

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:05.830
we once again have the problem of that

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:09.210
pawn So the move h3 comes to mind as a

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.570
candidate move in order to meet e6 with

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:13.890
bishop e3 Unfortunately, Black can fly

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.230
through a different airport, Black can

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:17.350
play knight to d5, and it's essentially

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:19.470
the same effect, where this bishop is

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.730
destabilized, so I actually don't think

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:22.990
that we should go for that approach.

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:27.410
Now, a3, e6, b4 fails tactically,

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.670
because Black will play a, b, c, b, and

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:30.690
knight takes b4.

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:32.650
You should know this from, you know,

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.230
the Queen's Gambit and other openings

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:36.130
where you get this pawn, this type of

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:37.210
pawn that you're trying to defend.

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.410
So, I actually think we should take a

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:41.870
more indirect approach.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:44.790
One of the drawbacks of the move a5 is

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:46.510
that it weakens the b5 square.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:49.030
So you might say, okay, well then let's

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:49.950
play bishop b5.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:52.410
But bishop b5 has limited value because

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:53.770
black can just play e6.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:57.110
What I like is a very Alepyn-esque type

00:03:57.110 --> 00:03:58.770
approach to this position that involves

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:00.310
trying to get the knight to b5.

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.750
And that seems very promising to me

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.090
because from b5, the knight will not

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:08.570
only be aiming at c7 but in the event

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:10.310
that black pushes the e pawn which is

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:12.330
very likely and let's play the move

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:15.210
knight a3 as i'm talking about it once

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:18.730
black plays e6 or e5 and we respond

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:21.070
with knight to b5 we can also stick the

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:23.850
knight onto d6 and what could happen

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.770
then is a situation where black takes

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.630
on d6 and we take back securing the

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:30.050
bishop pair and getting control of the

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:31.570
dark squares and Even if, in the

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.990
subsequent complications, black wins

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.510
that pawn on d6 back, I still think

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.170
that we're going to get the upper hand

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:39.530
in the resulting endgame.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.070
So, knight a3 is a move that should be

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:45.130
on your radar if you're experienced in

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:45.590
the alepen.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:48.230
I mean, we've had plenty of games where

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:49.790
I made this move in the middle game,

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:52.050
and it makes sometimes more sense to

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:53.450
people when there's a black queen on

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:56.470
d5, because then you can more clearly

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:58.310
see the effect of the move knight to b5.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:00.250
But even without the queen, knight to

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:02.190
c7 is still very much a threat.

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:06.550
So knight a3 is a nice flexible move.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:07.690
It's a developing move.

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:09.870
And we're kind of waiting for black to

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.370
put his cards on the table.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:14.740
All right.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.980
So it's a good sign that curious is

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:17.800
starting to think.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.900
Well, if black had played the immediate

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:22.920
e6, then of course we would have played b4.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:25.060
And that I think is one of the

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:25.760
theoretical lines.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:27.700
I'll open up the Chessable course after

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:30.980
the game to check in on what the actual

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.320
main line is, according to the authors.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:33.500
Knight to d5, okay.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:36.240
So a very understandable move.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:39.060
Black is trying to cover the c7 square.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.740
But just like the bishop on e3 is quite

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:44.000
unstable, so is the knight on d5.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.340
Now some of you might instinctively

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:48.700
want to play c4 or bishop to c4.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:50.540
Both of which are reasonable moves.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.820
I like bishop c4 more because it's a

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:53.420
developing move.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:56.060
But the problem with bishop c4 is the

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:58.180
black can respond with e6 and defend

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.200
the knight and open up an attack on c5.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:03.240
And in that subsequent position, we

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.680
actually could trade on d5 and play the

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:06.960
move bishop e3, and then we've

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.960
basically permanently secured our extra

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.100
pawn on c5.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:11.980
So that's one option.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.300
One option is absolutely to play bishop

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:15.720
c4 here and chase the knight away.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.180
Of course, another option is to stick

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:19.760
to our guns and play knight b5.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:21.740
And this one I like even more, because

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:23.980
knight b5 creates a very devious threat

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:27.040
of c4, as well as bishop c4.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:29.240
And once this knight is dislodged,

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:30.820
white's knight travels to c7.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:33.240
And it may seem like a case of

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:34.800
one-move-itis, like, oh, is this really

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:35.800
such a serious threat?

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.100
Well, it actually is a very serious

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:40.300
threat because in order to defend

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:42.760
against it, black has to make one of

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:45.100
several major concessions.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.780
Black basically has to ruin either his

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:49.360
king safety by moving the king the

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:52.180
d-file or his ability to castle

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:53.740
queenside by moving the rook.

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:56.140
In either case black basically has to

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:58.540
waste a very valuable move just to

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:00.880
defend against the threat of knight c7.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:04.380
King d8 okay and now that we have

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:06.780
induced this particular move our knight

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:08.280
on b5 is also just chilling.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.240
We could also go at it from the other

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:14.160
side with knight to g5 and that's a

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:15.780
more common idea than you might think,

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:17.820
sticking the Knights on b5 and g5,

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:19.880
hitting the f7 and c7 squares.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:22.860
So knight g5 is a very interesting approach.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:24.980
Black will probably respond with knight

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:27.520
e5 in order to defend f7.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:32.240
And if we play f4 there, ooh, then

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:33.760
Black could try to counter-attack our

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:34.740
Knight with h6.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:36.540
I'll show that line after the game.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:37.840
So that's one way we could play.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:39.560
We could also play this more

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:41.120
straightforwardly, just developing the

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.640
Bishop with tempo, Bishop c4.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:45.360
Looks like a great move as well.

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:46.720
I like Bishop c4.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:48.800
I think it's simple and very strong,

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:50.660
solidifies our advantage.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:53.740
Knight g5 is the move that you would

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:55.140
play if you wanted to win the game on

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:55.680
the spot.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:00.420
So knight g5, knight e5, c4, b4.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:02.140
I'm trying to calculate that line out.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:05.740
It's a little bit hard to assess how

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:08.020
good that is for white, in contrast to

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:09.940
just the simpler Bishop c4.

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:14.930
Knight g5, very very tempting move,

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:17.470
let's go for it, let's go knight g5,

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:19.410
I'm going for the throat this game,

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:22.030
sorry curious, I'm going for the

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:24.310
throat, just intuitively this feels

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:26.810
like it has to be the right move, just

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:28.510
intuitively it feels like knight g5

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:30.430
squeezes the most out of the position,

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:33.170
and it's not such a big commitment, you

00:08:33.170 --> 00:08:34.690
shouldn't think of a move like this

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:36.190
like a path of no return, okay it

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:38.610
doesn't work out, big deal, we're not

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:41.230
introducing any permanent weaknesses in

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:43.490
into our position, we're just creating

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:46.250
a threat, and knights, unlike pawns, do

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:47.030
move backwards.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:53.400
Okay, and bishop e6 immediately

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:55.580
justifies our move, immediately,

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:59.180
because now we have the standing option

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:03.000
of taking on e6, and to say that it

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.460
ruins black's pawn structure is

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:06.120
basically to say nothing, it just

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:08.760
permanently destroys any semblance of

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.600
normalcy that black has within the pawn structure.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.480
So I'm taking those doubled e-pawns any

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:16.400
day of the week, particularly

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:18.720
Wednesday, which it right now is at the

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:19.480
time of recording.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.960
Now, if you're a pedant, then you might

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:25.900
say, well, I don't have to take on e6 immediately.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:27.380
I can just sort of chill in this

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:29.080
position because the bishop's not going anywhere.

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:31.800
But that doesn't mean you always have

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:32.560
to do that.

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:34.620
Just because you don't have to rush a

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:36.900
move doesn't necessarily mean that you

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:39.000
shouldn't play it if you can avoid playing.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.020
And I don't see a reason for us to keep

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:42.020
this knight on g5.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:43.760
It's not doing all that much.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:46.740
And we might save a tempo on a

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:48.200
different move, but I think we should

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:49.880
play it simple and just take on e6

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:50.860
while we have the chance.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:55.960
So now we have a choice of different

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:57.000
squares for this bishop.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.660
And it's pretty self-evident to me that

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:01.180
our next step involves bringing this

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:02.000
bishop out.

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.260
Now the pawn on e6 is now one of

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:06.720
Black's main weaknesses and we can

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:08.040
attack it in several different ways.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:09.800
we can attack it from a rook that would

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:10.740
come to e1.

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:12.600
But I think I see an even more

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:15.160
effective way of really targeting this

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:17.520
pawn and solving several problems at

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:18.600
once, which is a relatively

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.600
unconventional form of development,

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:22.740
which is why I think some people might

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:24.760
struggle to visualize this idea.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.480
It might not occur naturally to you.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:29.580
And yet I think it's quite strong.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:32.860
Now, yeah, to get the Bishop onto like

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:35.000
either the h3 or g4 square.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:37.380
So there's two ways to do it.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:39.220
You can play bishop e2, bishop g4,

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:40.860
which is excellent, or you could play

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:44.000
g3, bishop h3, which I call the hyper

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:46.940
fianchetto because, you know, you're,

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:48.120
you're moving the bishop in that

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:50.260
direction, but you're not fianchettoing it.

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:54.880
Both options are totally viable, but I

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:56.780
like the look of g3 just because it's a

00:10:56.780 --> 00:10:58.040
little bit more elegant.

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:01.020
Yeah, let's go g3.

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:04.040
What about c4 to fork?

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:06.200
