And again it's not easy to play.

00:22:14.370 --> 00:22:17.110
Bishop e7, most popular move, and you

00:22:17.110 --> 00:22:18.810
just develop your pieces, bishop e2.

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:21.990
You just develop your pieces, castles,

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:25.330
castles, and as usual, if black ever

00:22:25.330 --> 00:22:27.810
plays e5, white's response is always

00:22:27.810 --> 00:22:28.630
going to be the same.

00:22:29.290 --> 00:22:30.990
If black ever plays e5, your knight

00:22:30.990 --> 00:22:32.910
goes back to b5, and again you get the

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:35.070
situation where the knight on a6 is

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:36.930
essentially out of commission, and now

00:22:36.930 --> 00:22:38.910
the pawn on e5 is another additional

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:41.290
weakness that black has be saddled with.

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:43.610
Hopefully this is making sense, just a

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:45.390
little bit of an overview of the main lines.

00:22:45.830 --> 00:22:47.370
Now let's get to the move that happened

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:48.590
in the game, knight to c6.

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:52.690
So d takes c5 is far from the only

00:22:52.690 --> 00:22:54.650
move, although it is the engine's

00:22:54.650 --> 00:22:56.350
choice at a very high depth.

00:22:56.350 --> 00:22:57.810
So let's get straight to that move.

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:00.170
Now it should be pointed out that queen

00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:02.990
takes c5 does not solve black's problems.

00:23:03.050 --> 00:23:04.510
As I pointed out, this gives white

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:07.170
rapid development and here the best

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:09.770
move is actually not Bishop e3, even

00:23:09.770 --> 00:23:10.790
though I said it during the game.

00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:14.330
I forgot the best move yet again, yet

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:16.670
again, is to play the immediate Knight

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:19.750
a3 heading straight for b5.

00:23:19.790 --> 00:23:21.570
Black can lose this really, really

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:23.170
quickly if he's not careful.

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:25.430
So a couple of illustrative lines,

00:23:25.950 --> 00:23:29.190
let's say Black plays e6, then our

00:23:29.190 --> 00:23:31.910
Knight jumps into b5 and already Black

00:23:31.910 --> 00:23:34.430
is in big trouble because if Black

00:23:34.430 --> 00:23:37.950
plays Knight to d5, then white wins on

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:39.730
the spot with the move c4.

00:23:40.010 --> 00:23:41.850
Same concept as in the game because

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:43.790
we're threatening the knight on d5 and

00:23:43.790 --> 00:23:45.370
if black counterattacks remember that

00:23:45.370 --> 00:23:47.470
you're also hitting the other knight so

00:23:47.470 --> 00:23:48.610
here you win on the spot.

00:23:49.270 --> 00:23:51.090
So black has to make a super awkward

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:53.230
move and kind of contort himself in

00:23:53.230 --> 00:23:56.850
order to defend the c7 square upon

00:23:56.850 --> 00:24:00.330
which we can play queen a4 and bring

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:02.150
the queen out and Black can't go here

00:24:02.150 --> 00:24:02.870
because of the fork.

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:06.570
So constantly Black is stumbling on his

00:24:06.570 --> 00:24:08.910
own feet here because of how cramped

00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:10.390
the position is.

00:24:10.930 --> 00:24:13.610
And after a6, Bf4, the game is over

00:24:13.610 --> 00:24:15.930
because we have too many threats, Nc7

00:24:15.930 --> 00:24:17.270
and Nd6.

00:24:17.670 --> 00:24:21.390
So this is a already very nasty position.

00:24:22.290 --> 00:24:26.350
If Black plays a6 in order to protect

00:24:26.350 --> 00:24:31.850
the b5 square, This is a very common situation.

00:24:32.930 --> 00:24:36.150
B5 is protected, but now C4 is

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:37.050
incredibly weak.

00:24:37.270 --> 00:24:39.290
So now we can go Bishop e3.

00:24:39.950 --> 00:24:42.590
Let's say the Queen swings over to h5.

00:24:42.910 --> 00:24:45.490
Now we can play Knight c4 and our

00:24:45.490 --> 00:24:46.990
Knight is heading for b6.

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:48.550
Again, this is very, very juicy.

00:24:49.550 --> 00:24:51.170
So no matter how you slice it, and you

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:52.450
can look at this on your own a little

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:54.770
bit more, there is also the move e5

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:56.590
here, which is a little bit more

00:24:56.590 --> 00:24:59.130
ambitious than e6, it happens pretty often.

00:24:59.270 --> 00:25:01.550
We play knight b5 a lot of GM games

00:25:01.550 --> 00:25:02.470
here Queen e7.

00:25:02.690 --> 00:25:05.430
This is one of the main lines and Here

00:25:05.430 --> 00:25:07.190
according to the engine white is

00:25:07.190 --> 00:25:08.990
slightly better But more importantly

00:25:08.990 --> 00:25:10.650
the position is very easy to play you

00:25:10.650 --> 00:25:13.830
play bishop e3 and if black plays a6

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:15.830
Your response here is always going to

00:25:15.830 --> 00:25:19.670
be the same queen to a4 Very important

00:25:19.670 --> 00:25:21.930
resource that pins the pawn to the rook

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:24.190
and you might say well Why is that

00:25:24.190 --> 00:25:26.010
dangerous black can just move the rook

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:27.570
well if black moves the rook you

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:30.790
actually castle queen side And this is

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:32.370
the line I show to anybody who says the

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:33.630
elephant is a boring opening.

00:25:34.190 --> 00:25:36.750
You sac the knight on b5 in order to

00:25:36.750 --> 00:25:38.790
get rapid development and a huge attack.

00:25:39.430 --> 00:25:42.630
Bishop takes b5, Bishop d7, defending

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:43.090
the knight.

00:25:43.750 --> 00:25:45.670
And of course, you make a very calm

00:25:45.670 --> 00:25:47.090
move, very important, just because

00:25:47.090 --> 00:25:48.910
you've sacrificed a piece doesn't mean

00:25:48.910 --> 00:25:51.450
that every subsequent move has to be a

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:53.330
sacrifice or has to be something forcing.

00:25:53.330 --> 00:25:55.690
You just bring your other rook into the

00:25:55.690 --> 00:25:58.210
game and that extra piece is the

00:25:58.210 --> 00:26:00.490
difference between Success and failure

00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:02.090
and the attack black is paralyzed black

00:26:02.090 --> 00:26:04.450
has no moves Let's say the black tries

00:26:04.450 --> 00:26:06.670
to bring his bishop out with g6.

00:26:06.830 --> 00:26:08.450
Well now it's time for more tactics

00:26:08.450 --> 00:26:12.730
Knight takes e5 bang Knight takes e5

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:15.550
and now bishop g5 creating another pin

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:18.310
Creating another pin and your pieces

00:26:18.310 --> 00:26:19.550
are all just I mean you're just gonna

00:26:19.550 --> 00:26:22.090
massacre black here in the center And

00:26:22.090 --> 00:26:25.750
then after Bg7, you have two pins, this

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:27.830
is one, and this is the other.

00:26:28.150 --> 00:26:30.330
So both of black's knights are pinned,

00:26:30.610 --> 00:26:33.450
which means you have Rxd7 winning the game.

00:26:33.950 --> 00:26:35.390
Really, really pretty line.

00:26:36.130 --> 00:26:37.350
Really, really pretty line.

00:26:38.590 --> 00:26:41.550
Knight takes d7, and bishop takes d7 check.

00:26:42.450 --> 00:26:44.310
And then and only then do you recapture

00:26:44.310 --> 00:26:44.690
the queen.

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:49.240
So it's a cool line, and it gives you a

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:51.120
sense of how promising this can be.

00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:55.860
And that's why I think King takes d1 is

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:58.520
really the only viable continuation for

00:26:58.520 --> 00:27:00.000
black from a practical standpoint.

00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:03.120
And here we get into some very

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:04.000
theoretical waters.

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:06.040
I don't want to bog you down with too

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:08.460
much exposition of the theory because

00:27:08.460 --> 00:27:09.580
you can study it yourself.

00:27:10.340 --> 00:27:11.420
And, you know, I know that a lot of

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:13.640
newer players are watching me, so it's

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:14.960
probably boring as it is.

00:27:15.220 --> 00:27:16.760
I'll just give you a sense of what the

00:27:16.760 --> 00:27:18.100
main lines are and why black

00:27:18.100 --> 00:27:19.380
experiences problems.

00:27:19.460 --> 00:27:22.060
So, on chess space, the main move is

00:27:22.060 --> 00:27:25.000
e5, and it's closely followed by Bg4.

00:27:25.060 --> 00:27:28.420
I actually think a5 is not a bad move.

00:27:29.560 --> 00:27:32.820
It's been played, but it does, you

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:34.880
know, it gives white a very hefty advantage.

00:27:35.860 --> 00:27:38.720
And my response, by the way, Na3 is not best.

00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:41.560
Apparently, after a5, the best move is,

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:45.570
in fact, to play the simple Bb5, and

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:49.350
then to meet Bd7, just sort of standard play.

00:27:49.450 --> 00:27:51.950
The engine actually likes ah, okay.

00:27:51.950 --> 00:27:52.170
Okay.

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:52.430
Okay.

00:27:52.430 --> 00:27:53.970
There's a detail that I missed

00:27:53.970 --> 00:27:55.930
according to the engine you go rookie

00:27:55.930 --> 00:28:00.910
one E6 Bishop e3 and 95 it turns out is

00:28:00.910 --> 00:28:03.030
not very scary because you can just

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:06.030
park the bishop on d4 So this is an

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:07.430
important detail you can put the bishop

00:28:07.430 --> 00:28:10.170
on d4 and if black ever takes it Then

00:28:10.170 --> 00:28:11.670
of course, you can rectify your pawn

00:28:11.670 --> 00:28:13.530
structure by taking back with a C pawn

00:28:13.530 --> 00:28:17.190
and now you're just up a full pawn so

00:28:17.250 --> 00:28:19.410
Yeah, but I still think after knight

00:28:19.410 --> 00:28:21.550
a3, it's all about the practical

00:28:21.550 --> 00:28:23.310
difficulty of playing a position like this.

00:28:23.430 --> 00:28:24.850
I think black's position becomes very

00:28:24.850 --> 00:28:26.710
difficult to play just because you have

00:28:26.710 --> 00:28:29.530
so many holes, and you have to be ultra

00:28:29.530 --> 00:28:31.410
precise in order to keep everything secure.

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:34.310
So instead, the two most popular moves

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:35.610
are e5 and bishop g4.

00:28:35.750 --> 00:28:37.850
In response to e5, white of course

00:28:37.850 --> 00:28:38.810
plays b4.

00:28:39.950 --> 00:28:41.870
And this is already very, very unpleasant.

00:28:42.390 --> 00:28:46.210
If black plays e4, then you can always

00:28:46.210 --> 00:28:47.990
swing the knight into the square that

00:28:47.990 --> 00:28:49.490
is now open, the d4 square.

00:28:50.330 --> 00:28:53.410
So the most popular move here after b4

00:28:53.410 --> 00:28:55.970
is bishop f5.

00:28:56.270 --> 00:28:57.610
But bishop f5, okay, it's not

00:28:57.610 --> 00:28:59.070
particularly dangerous, you can just

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:02.410
play bishop e3, which is the best move.

00:29:02.850 --> 00:29:05.410
And if black castles queenside, then

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:07.530
your king slides over to c1, which is a

00:29:07.530 --> 00:29:09.550
very safe square, and it's already plus minus.

00:29:09.550 --> 00:29:11.370
We're following a grandmaster game

00:29:11.370 --> 00:29:14.850
here, Barylovich against Moisenko from

00:29:14.850 --> 00:29:18.170
2007, and the best move here is just to

00:29:18.170 --> 00:29:19.470
bring the other bishop out to B5.

00:29:19.530 --> 00:29:21.950
You are not afraid of allowing Nxc3,

00:29:21.950 --> 00:29:23.630
because you're a pawn up, and again,

00:29:23.710 --> 00:29:25.630
Black is experiencing major problems

00:29:25.630 --> 00:29:27.850
with finding a suitable developing

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:28.550
square for the bishop.

00:29:29.570 --> 00:29:31.310
There's also a Gawain-Jones game here,

00:29:31.310 --> 00:29:32.970
titled Arena on Leech as well, so White

00:29:32.970 --> 00:29:34.210
actually played a lot of theory here in

00:29:34.210 --> 00:29:34.870
a bullet game.

00:29:36.990 --> 00:29:39.850
f6 is what happened and now white can

00:29:39.850 --> 00:29:41.610
improve the placement of our king with

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:44.050
King b2 then you can play Knight bd2

00:29:44.050 --> 00:29:46.450
rook d1 again black has no compensation

00:29:46.450 --> 00:29:48.430
for the pawn and remember that anytime

00:29:48.430 --> 00:29:50.310
black tries to cut into the pawn chain

00:29:50.310 --> 00:29:53.190
you can always just play a3 once

00:29:53.190 --> 00:29:55.790
black's rook leaves a8 you can just

00:29:55.790 --> 00:29:59.650
meet a5 with a3 so that's one line the

00:29:59.650 --> 00:30:01.610
other move that occurs very often is

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:02.550
Bishop to g4.

00:30:03.970 --> 00:30:06.050
This would probably be my choice if I

00:30:06.050 --> 00:30:07.170
knew nothing about the position.

00:30:07.390 --> 00:30:09.630
Here you take the opportunity to bring

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:11.470
your Bishop out to e3.

00:30:13.050 --> 00:30:14.910
Now the main move is knight to e5, as

00:30:14.910 --> 00:30:17.090
we discussed during the game.

00:30:17.350 --> 00:30:19.430
And the engine move is bishop c4.

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:21.750
This is kind of sort of an engine-y

00:30:21.750 --> 00:30:23.850
line that is worth just memorizing,

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:26.010
because it turns out that after knight

00:30:26.010 --> 00:30:28.450
takes e3, f takes e3, white's play

00:30:28.450 --> 00:30:32.110
hinges on a very subtlety, which is

00:30:32.110 --> 00:30:35.670
that after e5 you do not play b4 in

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:36.290
this position.

00:30:37.030 --> 00:30:40.210
The reason b4 is bad is because after

00:30:40.210 --> 00:30:43.210
e4 h3 we had a very similar position

00:30:43.210 --> 00:30:45.310
where White's bishop was still on f1.

00:30:45.930 --> 00:30:47.130
What's the chief difference?

00:30:47.410 --> 00:30:49.050
Well the chief difference is that now

00:30:49.050 --> 00:30:51.050
black can play f takes g2 and this is

00:30:51.050 --> 00:30:52.070
very very unpleasant.

00:30:52.450 --> 00:30:54.510
This gives black additional tempe that

00:30:54.510 --> 00:30:55.190
he can use.

00:30:55.190 --> 00:30:56.650
Black is here.

00:30:56.650 --> 00:30:59.170
Instead what you do here is you play

00:30:59.170 --> 00:31:00.550
the immediate h3.

00:31:00.750 --> 00:31:04.030
You sacrifice the c5 pawn back.

00:31:04.850 --> 00:31:06.170
Now it looks like black takes it and

00:31:06.170 --> 00:31:07.490
black is completely fine, but

00:31:07.490 --> 00:31:08.670
appearances are misleading.

00:31:09.030 --> 00:31:11.170
I analyze this position and white is

00:31:11.170 --> 00:31:12.530
significantly better here.

00:31:12.910 --> 00:31:14.370
This can be a little bit hard to explain.

00:31:14.430 --> 00:31:15.850
It's about plus over minus.

00:31:16.190 --> 00:31:18.370
It's 0.7 in this position.

00:31:19.330 --> 00:31:21.010
And I would say there's three reasons

00:31:21.010 --> 00:31:22.750
that white is better in this position.

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:26.130
The first is white's control over light

00:31:26.130 --> 00:31:28.430
squares is more meaningful than black's

00:31:28.430 --> 00:31:29.550
control over dark squares.

00:31:30.270 --> 00:31:31.930
Notice that a lot of the critical

00:31:31.930 --> 00:31:33.730
important squares in the center are all

00:31:33.730 --> 00:31:34.490
light squares.

00:31:34.970 --> 00:31:36.630
Whereas black's bishop is sort of

00:31:36.630 --> 00:31:38.890
biting on granite on E3, and white's

00:31:38.890 --> 00:31:40.470
king is a very reliable defender.

00:31:40.770 --> 00:31:43.490
Yeah, E3 is technically a weakness, but

00:31:43.490 --> 00:31:45.830
black cannot reinforce the pressure on

00:31:45.830 --> 00:31:46.290
that pawn.

00:31:47.310 --> 00:31:49.030
Reason number two is that white's pawn

00:31:49.030 --> 00:31:50.990
majority is more mobile than Black's

00:31:50.990 --> 00:31:51.430
pawn majority.

00:31:51.650 --> 00:31:53.690
Comparing pawn majorities is a very

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:55.190
good way of understanding the position

00:31:55.190 --> 00:31:56.030
at a deeper level.

00:31:56.090 --> 00:31:57.330
White is a 3 on 2.

00:31:57.690 --> 00:31:59.730
Black is a 4 on 3, but that doesn't matter.

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:01.930
What matters is that White's pawns are

00:32:01.930 --> 00:32:04.670
already ready to rumble forward B4, A4,

00:32:04.670 --> 00:32:07.810
A5, and if Black plays A5, then he

00:32:07.810 --> 00:32:09.810
creates a gaping weakness on B5.

00:32:10.450 --> 00:32:11.710
On the other hand, Black's pawn

00:32:11.710 --> 00:32:14.330
majority doesn't roll forward very easily.

00:32:14.430 --> 00:32:16.850
Black can play F5, But black can't

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:18.430
create a passed pawn very easily, and

00:32:18.430 --> 00:32:20.190
black's pawn movements aren't very meaningful.

00:32:21.170 --> 00:32:22.950
The third reason is that white's knight

00:32:22.950 --> 00:32:25.170
has better prospects than its counterpart.

00:32:25.650 --> 00:32:27.290
Notice that black's knight on c6 is

00:32:27.290 --> 00:32:28.850
completely restricted by these two

00:32:28.850 --> 00:32:30.510
pawns, highlighted in yellow.

00:32:30.970 --> 00:32:32.890
They're controlling all of the knight's

00:32:32.890 --> 00:32:34.110
potential advancing squares.

00:32:34.290 --> 00:32:36.270
White's knight has many different paths

00:32:36.270 --> 00:32:36.930
that it can take.

00:32:36.950 --> 00:32:39.170
In particular, it has very easy access

00:32:39.170 --> 00:32:41.390
to the e4 square, and later on in the

00:32:41.390 --> 00:32:42.830
game could potentially access the

00:32:42.830 --> 00:32:44.130
outpost on d5.

00:32:44.810 --> 00:32:48.290
So that is, in sort of verbally, why

00:32:48.290 --> 00:32:48.830
white is better.

00:32:48.910 --> 00:32:51.650
Now, how is the game likely to unfold?

00:32:52.090 --> 00:32:54.590
Well, if black plays f5, then the

00:32:54.590 --> 00:32:56.070
engine move here actually is

00:32:56.070 --> 00:32:57.810
immediately to start pushing the pawns

00:32:57.810 --> 00:32:59.330
forward with a4.

00:32:59.770 --> 00:33:02.150
Black plays, let's say, rook d8, for

00:33:02.150 --> 00:33:02.910
example, right?

00:33:02.910 --> 00:33:04.110
Just making neutral moves.

00:33:04.530 --> 00:33:07.310
And b4, you just start grabbing space

00:33:07.310 --> 00:33:09.070
and pushing black's bishop away and

00:33:09.070 --> 00:33:09.770
pieces away.

00:33:10.310 --> 00:33:11.850
And now a5.

00:33:12.310 --> 00:33:14.710
A lot of people, newer players, would

00:33:14.710 --> 00:33:16.030
be tempted to go B5.

00:33:16.370 --> 00:33:18.350
Who can tell me why this is a bad move?

00:33:18.690 --> 00:33:19.850
And if you're watching on YouTube and

00:33:19.850 --> 00:33:20.910
you're sort of newer to chess and

00:33:20.910 --> 00:33:22.410
you're a little bit lost, this is a

00:33:22.410 --> 00:33:24.130
good opportunity to review some of the

00:33:24.130 --> 00:33:24.910
positional basics.

00:33:25.050 --> 00:33:27.930
Why is B5 bad, but A5 is good?

00:33:31.260 --> 00:33:33.220
Yeah, it's because of Knight A5, and

00:33:33.220 --> 00:33:34.540
what's happened is that you have

00:33:34.540 --> 00:33:36.540
allowed Black to establish a complete

00:33:36.540 --> 00:33:38.620
blockade on the dark squares, and

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:40.060
because you don't have a dark squared

00:33:40.060 --> 00:33:42.420
bishop, your pawns are now fully paralyzed.

00:33:42.420 --> 00:33:44.860
So don't get blockaded like that, push

00:33:44.860 --> 00:33:46.960
the other pawn first, now you're trying

00:33:46.960 --> 00:33:50.140
to go a6 in order to soften up Black's

00:33:50.140 --> 00:33:51.100
knight on c6.

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:53.600
Now I hope my explanations earlier were

00:33:53.600 --> 00:33:54.600
somewhat understandable.

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:56.620
And this is a type of position that

00:33:56.620 --> 00:33:58.100
occurred in one of the Nepo Ding games

00:33:58.100 --> 00:33:59.800
where Black is now saddled with

00:33:59.800 --> 00:34:02.180
permanent weaknesses of his pawns.

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:03.740
And White's knight potentially has

00:34:03.740 --> 00:34:04.800
access to c5.

00:34:05.020 --> 00:34:06.840
And Black can't castle because White's

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:07.800
controlling the light squares.

00:34:09.080 --> 00:34:12.940
So, you know, it's, it's good not to be

00:34:12.940 --> 00:34:14.480
dismissive and just say, Oh, this is

00:34:14.480 --> 00:34:15.560
too much for me to understand.

00:34:15.700 --> 00:34:16.760
I'm too new to chess.

00:34:17.540 --> 00:34:20.040
You can understand how such positions operate.

00:34:20.060 --> 00:34:21.720
And that is exactly how you learn about

00:34:21.720 --> 00:34:23.560
positional chess, how you learn about

00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:25.460
maneuvering play and where pieces belong.

00:34:25.620 --> 00:34:27.440
It's by analyzing end games like this one.

00:34:27.820 --> 00:34:29.620
But the bottom line here is that you do

00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:32.980
sometimes sacrifice the C5 pawn back in

00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:35.640
order to gain certain positional assets.

00:34:35.780 --> 00:34:37.900
In this case, to reclaim the bishop

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:40.880
pair and to gain control over the light squares.

00:34:41.160 --> 00:34:44.300
By the way, if after h3, black refuses

00:34:44.300 --> 00:34:47.100
to take the knight, if black refuses to

00:34:47.100 --> 00:34:50.900
take the knight, the move here, just a

00:34:50.900 --> 00:34:52.180
moment, what is the move here?

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:55.660
Oh, it's just g4, bishop g6.

00:34:56.600 --> 00:34:58.620
And actually you go king-y2, so you

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:00.640
still give the pawn back, and then you

00:35:00.640 --> 00:35:02.560
go after black's bishop with knight h4.

00:35:02.560 --> 00:35:03.960
and then you bring the other knight

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:05.760
out, and again, your control over the

00:35:05.760 --> 00:35:07.500
light squares, as well as the prospect

00:35:07.500 --> 00:35:09.560
of pushing your queenside pawns, gives

00:35:09.560 --> 00:35:12.280
white an advantage here, despite appearances.

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:14.720
By appearance, this should be equal,

00:35:14.720 --> 00:35:16.260
but because of these weak squares,

00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:17.880
white is actually better here.

00:35:18.980 --> 00:35:21.640
So very instructive positional ideas

00:35:21.640 --> 00:35:24.480
here on top of the sort of specifics of

00:35:24.480 --> 00:35:25.140
the variation.

00:35:25.700 --> 00:35:27.060
Last thing I would say is that you

00:35:27.060 --> 00:35:29.100
should not be afraid of getting your

00:35:29.100 --> 00:35:30.760
kingside pawn structure ruined, because

00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:33.340
here you also get the bishop And white,

00:35:33.460 --> 00:35:35.440
black has no easy path to reclaiming

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:37.600
the c5 pawn, because you can always

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:38.820
reinforce it with b4.

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:42.020
And so I would say that black is in a

00:35:42.020 --> 00:35:44.100
lot of practical trouble in this endgame.

00:35:44.540 --> 00:35:46.320
Of course, if black plays knight e4,

00:35:46.560 --> 00:35:48.560
trying to fork these two pawns, you

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:50.960
have bishop e3, and this is why this

00:35:50.960 --> 00:35:52.180
line works concretely.

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:54.680
You have this bishop e3 move to support

00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:55.800
both pawns at once.

00:35:57.460 --> 00:36:01.260
So in any case, a5, knight a3 is not

00:36:01.260 --> 00:36:04.060
the most precise move, and the reason

00:36:04.060 --> 00:36:06.000
apparently, well white is still better.

00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:09.200
Apparently bishop g4 chimp is the best

00:36:09.200 --> 00:36:09.820
move here.

00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:12.540
Just quickly prioritizing development

00:36:12.540 --> 00:36:14.880
rather than trying to sort of

00:36:14.880 --> 00:36:16.680
artificially defend the c7 square.

